  "enhanced_information": "The 15-year-old girl presents with abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, decreased appetite, and right lower quadrant tenderness with guarding, alongside an elevated leukocyte count. These symptoms are suggestive of an acute abdominal process, likely due to infection or inflammation. Bacterial mesenteric lymphadenitis is a common cause of such symptoms in this age group, particularly following viral infections or gastroenteritis. Pseudomembranous colitis is less likely given the absence of antibiotic history, and diverticular inflammation is rare in this demographic. Congenital anomalies of the omphalomesenteric duct are also uncommon. Lymphatic tissue hyperplasia could be a consideration but is not the most typical presentation. Therefore, bacterial mesenteric lymphadenitis is the most likely underlying cause of this patient's condition, supported by the clinical presentation and age group."
